goen's fonts, and where they came from
======================================

Three families, 109 woff2 files, 4.17 MiB. They are the files
Google Fonts serves, fetched once and vendored here. Nothing subsets them at
build time and nothing fetches them at run time: `go:embed` puts them in the
binary and assets/assets.go serves them from /static with a digest in the URL
and a year of immutable caching.

Licence
-------

All three are under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1, which requires the
licence and the copyright notice to travel with the font files wherever they
are redistributed. Each family directory carries its own OFL.txt, copied
verbatim from that family's directory in github.com/google/fonts, and those
files are embedded and served alongside the fonts rather than only living in
the repository:

  assets/fonts/noto-sans-tc/OFL.txt
    Copyright 2014-2021 Adobe (http://www.adobe.com/),
    with Reserved Font Name 'Source'
  assets/fonts/inter/OFL.txt
    Copyright 2020 The Inter Project Authors
    (https://github.com/rsms/inter)
  assets/fonts/jetbrains-mono/OFL.txt
    Copyright 2020 The JetBrains Mono Project Authors
    (https://github.com/JetBrains/JetBrainsMono)

The three texts are the same licence; they differ only in the copyright line
and in where the upstream wrapped it. Each is kept as its upstream ships it
rather than merged into one, so a diff against upstream is a diff and not an
argument about formatting. TestEveryFontFamilyCarriesItsLicence fails if a
family is ever added without one.

Nothing here is modified. The OFL permits bundling and redistribution of
unmodified files under the original names; goen changes no glyph, no name and
no metadata.


What is here, and why only this
-------------------------------

  Noto Sans TC   105 files   Han. Every subset Google splits the family into.
  Inter            2 files   latin and latin-ext.
  JetBrains Mono   2 files   latin and latin-ext.

Source and version, as fonts.gstatic.com paths record them:

  Noto Sans TC     /s/notosanstc/v39/
  Inter            /s/inter/v20/
  JetBrains Mono   /s/jetbrainsmono/v24/

Google revises a family under a new vN path, so a future fetch that lands on a
different version is a deliberate upgrade with a new set of digests, not a
silent drift.

All three are VARIABLE fonts with a 400-700 weight axis, so one file per subset
covers every weight goen paints. That is why there are 105 Noto Sans TC files
and not 420: the static build needs one file per subset per weight.

Inter and JetBrains Mono keep only their Latin subsets. Google also serves them
in Cyrillic, Greek and Vietnamese; goen paints none of those, and a character
outside the ranges here falls to the system stack exactly as it did before.

Noto Sans TC keeps all 105, including its Latin ones. A browser downloads a
subset only when it paints a character inside that subset's unicode-range, so
the count costs binary size and not bandwidth: a page of Latin text fetches one
file and a page of Han fetches the handful its characters fall in.

Newsreader and Space Grotesk are not here. The design system names them, goen's
own tokens override both, and a browser on a goen page has never downloaded
either — checked with Network.requestWillBeSent before any of this was removed.

How to regenerate
-----------------

Google serves a different woff2 when the family is revised, and its CSS is the
only place the unicode-ranges are published. To take a new revision:

  1. Fetch the three stylesheets with a browser's User-Agent, which is what
     makes Google answer with woff2 rather than ttf:

       curl -A "$BROWSER_UA" \
         'https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Noto+Sans+TC:wght@400..700&display=swap'
       curl -A "$BROWSER_UA" \
         'https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400..700&display=swap'
       curl -A "$BROWSER_UA" \
         'https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400..700&display=swap'

  2. For each @font-face, download the woff2 it names into the directory for
     its family, named after the subset comment above it (the unlabelled Noto
     Sans TC subsets are numbered cjk-000 upward, in the order Google lists
     them — the order is the unicode-range order and it is stable).

  3. Rewrite assets/css/app/fonts.css from the same stylesheets: one @font-face
     per file, carrying Google's own font-weight, font-style and unicode-range,
     `font-display: swap`, and a src of
     /static/fonts/<family>/<subset>.woff2?v=<first 12 hex of the file's sha256>.

  4. `go test ./assets/` — TestFontStylesheetMatchesTheEmbeddedFonts compares
     every digest in the sheet against the embedded file and fails on a file
     that no rule names.

Subsetting tool and version: NONE, and that is the point rather than an
omission. Every file here is a byte-for-byte copy of what fonts.gstatic.com
serves; Google runs the subsetting and publishes the resulting unicode-ranges
in its CSS, and copying both is the whole step. No fonttools, no pyftsubset, no
build step, nothing added to go.mod or the Makefile, and no way for a local
tool's version to change what a visitor is sent.

Fetched with Chrome 153's User-Agent on 2026-09-19 from fonts.googleapis.com
and fonts.gstatic.com. The User-Agent matters: Google answers an older one with
ttf and a different set of subsets.

